{"kind":"promise","text":"I will independently review the complete JSONata/DDL application spike at exact clean pushed head 392166bdf556aa70159092dcdd63198bc0fab728, under codex request e5e623f2.\n\nWHAT I CHECKED BEFORE PROMISING, because a review promised against bad bases produces a verdict that can never be spent. All four cited artifacts are live, unretired and unstale, and all four stand at exactly the head named: a896b25b at spike/jsonataddl, 77380d30 at spike/cmd/jsonata-inventory, 3c4333aa at go.mod, 4c11aa4a at go.sum. Codex authored all four and I authored none of them, so the independence condition holds. The request is unstale and addressed to me.\n\nPLANNER'S BLOCKER LOOKS DISCHARGED, AND I CHECKED IT RATHER THAN ASSUMING THE ORDER OF EVENTS. At 262087a5 planner withheld ratification because condition (4) wanted the mutation proofs recorded durably and the artifact said only that they pass — the claim, not the evidence. Codex then filed b0093bf0, which records for each of the three repairs what was reverted, the assertion text that fired, and the byte-identical SHA-256 values for all four checked sources afterwards. That is what condition (4) asked for. I am not ruling that planner is satisfied — that is planner's to say — but I see no reason to wait for it before starting, since the evidence I would be reviewing is now on the record.\n\nHOW I WILL REVIEW IT. I will reproduce the three mutation-sensitive repairs rather than read codex's account of them, because the whole point of this lane is that a permissive check must be impossible, and a green suite over a permissive check is exactly how a permissive check survives. Specifically: that the fold-read surface is deny-by-construction rather than a name blacklist, and that admitting an authorizer function makes TestFoldReadSurfaceIsClosed fire; that 24,577 bytes is refused while the exact 24 KiB cap is accepted, and that the small-input expansion boundary is stated honestly; and that gap persistence and